A||||14||fierospeeder||Common Car Breakdowns for make and model||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||03-02-2001||12:25 AM||||I will find this interesting and others will also.  <BR>The idea is to write down your experience with any car and what is something that broke down a lot with the car.  The only thing you cant do is to be bias on it.  (example, i never changed the oil so i blew a rod so all fords suck)<P>Heres mine<P>74 vw beetle, i haven't really driven the car but heres some common problems.  The cars are really prone to rust, its the worst thing to find on a vw beelte when buying one.  Engine problems are minimal when rebuilding one costs 200 bucks.  Engines tend to leak oil from old gaskets.<P>84 pontiac fiero, sensors going out with age, sparkplug wires tend to crack from extreme heat,  Prone to oil leaks from the pan because the timing gear had no bolts to connect it to the pan, which may cause belts to break when oil leaked on them.  Oil leaks from old gaskets.  <P>87 ford thunderbird turbo l-4<BR>a4ld transmission, numerous problems, bad trans ford used on the car, when buying this car get the 5 speed which is the best manual trans they make.  Power steering lines tend to leak with age on most rear wheel drive cars that ford+lincoln+mercury made in the 80s.  so called "ignition module" can go out due to heat, "i never had a problem yet.  Sensor plugs tend to crack with heat and age and some sensors do go out with age.  I find that the gauges on the dash are inaccurate.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tweek||03-02-2001||04:10 AM||||an 87 and an 88 Grand Am, both with Iron Ducks, both blew at about 150-160K. Now my 84 Fiero only has 90K, so hopefully, with care it will last...<P>'72 Jaguar XJ6 (Resto...) Rust. Oil Leaks. More Rust.<P>'83 Pontiac Parisienne with a 305. I did learn something here... when a car burns/leaks oil, do remember to check it every now and again. The motor in this car seized up one day while driving... checked the oil and none showed up (less than 2 qts.) "Oops.. forgot about that"<P>Late 80's Toyota pickup owned by grandfather. Only comment when letting me borrow it, "Remember to check the oil, it burns a bit of it" Next day, before I had a chance to, pieces of engine all over road, not to mention 1 Ft wife oil strip for several hundred ft.<P>84 Camaro Berlinetta. 2.8V6 Auto... automatic trans fluid pump went, leaving me with no forward gears.<P><BR>With all this experience I now learn to check most fluids several times a week.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||03-02-2001||06:39 AM||||All chrysler 3.0 V6, especially minivan. Poor head design/quality. Very easily cracked, valve guides/seals go out very fast(idle smoking).<P>All Jags, especially lower front fenders, rear edge of trunklid, top of qtr panel where it meets bottom of roof pillar.<P>Porsches, especially all 911's, bottom of front fenders, all around headlite buckets.914's center floorpan (car will break clear in two)<P>All Chrysler 'K' cars, rust on top of front fenders, there was a recall where they did give you free new fenders, but no labor.<P>All Datsun 240-280Z's, quarter panel rustout.<P>Ford Aerostar vans, all years, Rocker panel rustout. They turn to nothing clear into pass compartment floorpan. Cost over $500 per side to replace.<P>Any cars with Aero style headlites, some of the headlite assbys cost as much as $500. each new.<P>Pantera lots of areas prone to rust, subframes and body<P>All Ford full size with ladder type frame. Frame rustout at bumper mounting points or ahead of rear wheel kickup.<P>All GM full size with ladder frame, frame rustout at bumper mounts.<P>   *im sure youve all seen them with rope or wire holding it up, or wooden bumpers!! Dont follow one in your Fiero, may end up pole vaulting over it when it falls off in front of you.<P>Thats a few of what ive run across over and over, feel free to email me about anycar you might be buying, Ive prob had to fix at least 1.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||03-02-2001||08:40 AM||||1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme Int. 2.8l V6<P>O2 sensor burnt from heat and age, 12 year old alternator went.  Transmission is beginning to show it's age(TH440T4, just kicks a little going into gear), engine mount bushings cracked after 12 years, they've been replaced.  Power steering pump blew a seal.  Oil sensor went, and oil pump was replaced with the sensors(pressure was a little low).  All in all, any mechanic that looked at it said the engine is in great shape for having 400,000km(250,000+ miles) on it.  In my opinion the 2.8l was the best V6 GM made.<P>The biggest problem on this model of car is that this one was made in Georgia and the bolts holding the wheel assembly to the a frame were substandard for Canada and corroded from salt, lucky thing I was only going about 20kph when I went around and corner and had to stop because the wheel popped off the ball joint.  Broke the CV joint away from the engine.  Mechanic said my parents were lucky I was smart enough to jack the car up quickly or it would've bent the hell out of the entire assembly and it would've had to been replaced.  $800 and 2 days later the car was back on the road.<P>Only rust you have to check for is on the spare tire well, where the bolt assembly for holding the tire in the well is.  The only rust this car has is from where paint has chipped away and even then, it's only on the surface, none has eatten through.  It just needs a good sanding and repaint.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||87ECO5||03-02-2001||09:05 AM||||Not sure if this is really common but I've owned two J-bodies (1994 Cavalier and 1992 Sunbird) and both had problems with the wiper system.  In all cases, the wipers would just stop working (of course, it always happened during a rain storm).  The Cavalier was sent to the dealership three times (under warranty) for this.  The Sunbird, strangely enough, corrected itself when the car was turned off and back on.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tweek||03-02-2001||11:41 AM||||p4n1c, I have to agree with you. My father got a used 1987 ciera when it was 10 years old. Had about 200,000 mi. on it. drove it another 100,000. engine still ran great, car was too rusted to be repairable. Now its a parts car for the '91 Ciera (wagon) with about 215,000 miles. (with a 3.1) Not too flashy, but damn good cars with good motors.<BR>The only 2.8 that Ive seen that was known for trouble was the one in his 87 S-10. Not sure what it was that was wrong, (too young) but I remember lots of coolant in the oil, and the mechanic saying that year had some probs...||||reg||1
Z||000006||p4n1c||03-02-2001||11:57 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tweek:<BR><B>The only 2.8 that Ive seen that was known for trouble was the one in his 87 S-10. Not sure what it was that was wrong, (too young) but I remember lots of coolant in the oil, and the mechanic saying that year had some probs...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yup, the 2.8s in the S-10 weren't the most reliable, and coolant in the oil was one problem, I think there was a seal problem or something.  Can't remember.  My brother has an 87 Blazer S-10.  <P>I don't know if the 87 Ciera had the same engine, but the 2.8s in the Cutlass and Beretta, etc had DIS.  I scared some guy and his kid in the parking lot, we were having idling problems for a bit until it got fixed and when the ECM reads a drop below 400rpm it essentially restarts the engine.  Well, I was standing outside the car and noone was in it and they walked by and the engine restarted and rev'd up to about 4000rpm scared the crap out of them.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It has a little less growl then the Fiero(only dual pipe exhaust, instead of the duals).<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||03-02-2001||12:10 PM||||96 Ford Windstar: Tranny gave up after about 80k, but I did drive the ****  out of it (even popping it into gear from neutral), but we've had a lot of problems with fords.<P>96 Nissan Maxima: None. What a beaut!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>85 Fiero: Frame rust. Frame ended up snapping where a-arm connects (in rear)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||ray b||03-02-2001||01:24 PM||||corvairs leak oil from push rod tubes and break fan belts.<BR>BMW 4 piston brakes stick and are very $$$ to fix, al heads crack and warp esp pre 85 cars like 733.<BR>ford tauris motor mounts fail.recallled too late<BR>pontaic 455 heads are $$$ for 9.0 C/R or more with big valves.<BR>914 rust every where<BR>fiero wires at bulkhead can chafe and short causing fire as my 87 se did.<BR>old VW van tranfer cases suck.<BR>68 GTO rear frames rust away as do trunks floor. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||03-02-2001||05:19 PM||||93-97 Ford Probes with automatic transmissions.  These things are made out of paper!  Nearly everyone I have talked to who has owned one had the ATX go out before 60K miles!  The trans in my 97 died after only 11,000 miles, and no it wasn't driven really hard!  I love that new car warrenty!!<P>87 Fiero... coils and ignition modules.  For a while it was blowing them out about every year... thankfully it stopped doing that about 4 years ago.  Knock on wood!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||MoneypitGT||03-02-2001||10:32 PM||||Any Chrysler with the 604e tranny, had one in my '97 Cirrus, 1,200 miles, blew a hole in the side that you could fit you hand in. Replaced under warranty, 2,000 miles later, #2 went in, locked in 2nd gear. The 3rd was a charm especially after I started beating the piss out of the car, ran great up till I got rid of it last year with 53,000 on it. Oh and major water leaks, sometimes, leaked when it wanted too.||||reg||1
Z||000011||LarryB||03-03-2001||01:06 AM||||'83-'85 Nissan pickups.  Bed floor was attached to walls by rolling the edges together cushioned by thin rubber. Moisture was prone to collect in these joints and the beds would rust out prematurely. Nissan admitted to the design flaw and fixed it in the '86 redesign. <P>I know from personal experience about the problems with the 2.8 in my '87 S-10 Blazer, the Chevy dealer in Woburn MA by their own admission botched a minor repair job and the engine seized less than 30 miles later.  GM honored the 60,000 mile warranty (engine had 56,xxx miles on it) and had the engine replaced although the original dealer couldn't even do that properly. A 2nd dealership had to finish and fix the installation. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cooter||03-03-2001||02:27 AM||||Mercury Mystique- pretty much any interior and exterior falls off whenever it wants. Power steering pressure sensor goes out and causes weird engine problems. Rear wheel bearings must be made of lead- replaced twice and had began making noise again when the car was wrecked.<P>Honda CRV- outer front cv joints cause driveline vibrations. Both were replaced and less than 10,000 miles later it is doing it again and dealership says "it is normal" and will not replace them again on a 25,000 mile vehicle. All four Firestone tires grew knots in the tread and sidewalls and had to be replaced at out expense.<P>Late model full size Ford trucks with v6- front timing cover develops water leak into engine. Several of these engines are laying around back at the dealership with less than 5,000 miles on them.||||reg||1
Z||000013||kslish||03-03-2001||03:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>The biggest problem on this model of car is that this one was made in Georgia and the bolts holding the wheel assembly to the a frame were substandard for Canada and corroded from salt<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>GM just recalled my '89 Cutlass Supreme Coupe to replace all the front suspension bolts since the stock bolts suffered from the corrosion problem that you mentioned.  <P>It's been a great car and still drives like it was new (currently has 116,000 miles on it) other than the transmission going at 106,000 miles and I've had the roof and rear fender repainted since they suffered from the infamous "pealing paint" problem from that era.<P>Ken S.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||03-03-2001||05:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>'83-'85 Nissan pickups.  Bed floor was attached to walls by rolling the edges together cushioned by thin rubber. Moisture was prone to collect in these joints and the beds would rust out prematurely. Nissan admitted to the design flaw and fixed it in the '86 redesign. <P>I know from personal experience about the problems with the 2.8 in my '87 S-10 Blazer, the Chevy dealer in Woburn MA by their own admission botched a minor repair job and the engine seized less than 30 miles later.  GM honored the 60,000 mile warranty (engine had 56,xxx miles on it) and had the engine replaced although the original dealer couldn't even do that properly. A 2nd dealership had to finish and fix the installation. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mazda and TOyota pickups have same major problem.<P>||||reg||1
